export default "# A2UI Color Philosophy: The Patchwork Quilt\n\n## Breaking Expectations\n\n> \"This is not going to be a regular experience\"\n\nThe A2UI prompt console embraces **arbitrary, personalized color choices** as a design principle. Each prompt section has its own visual identity - not because it has to follow a design system, but because it **accentuates the individuality of each card**.\n\n## The Mixed Greens Aesthetic\n\nThose \"bizarre mixed greens\" that are so satisfying? They're intentional. They create a patchwork quilt of different prompts, different styles, different things. Each shade serves as a **visual identifier** that says:\n\n- \"This is the System Role section\" (one shade of green)\n- \"This is the Constraints area\" (a different green)\n- \"This is where Context lives\" (yet another green)\n\nIt's not uniform. It's not corporate. It's **alive**.\n\n## User Empowerment Through Color\n\n**\"People can pick their own colors. It's their prompt console.\"**\n\n### The Philosophy:\n- **Personalization over Uniformity**: Each user can have their own color scheme\n- **Individual Experience**: Your workspace should reflect YOUR mental model\n- **Arbitrary is Beautiful**: The colors don't need justification - they just need to work for YOU\n\n### Potential \"Problems\" That Are Actually Features:\n- One user has green for System Role, another has orange? **That's personalization.**\n- Sharing becomes visually different between users? **That's individuality.**\n- No consistent brand palette? **That's freedom.**\n\n## Visual Differentiation as UX\n\nThe patchwork approach serves real functional purposes:\n\n1. **Instant Recognition**: Different colors = different mental spaces\n2. **Cognitive Mapping**: Your brain associates colors with functions\n3. **Reduced Hunting**: No scanning for labels when colors tell the story\n4. **Personal Landmarks**: \"The blue section is where I put my examples\"\n\n## Implementation Ideas\n\n### Current State\n- Different sections have different green shades\n- Creates visual variety without being chaotic\n- Breaks the monotony of single-color interfaces\n\n### Future Possibilities\n- User-selectable color themes per section\n- Save personal color schemes\n- Share color schemes as \"prompt palettes\"\n- Random color generation for new sections\n- Mood-based color shifts\n\n## The Anti-Pattern We're Avoiding\n\nWe're explicitly NOT doing:\n- Everything the same shade of corporate blue\n- Rigid adherence to a \"design system\"\n- Accessibility at the cost of personality (we can have both!)\n- Uniform experiences that feel like spreadsheets\n\n## Quotes That Define This Philosophy\n\n> \"It just blows your mind. It gets rid of all your expectations.\"\n\n> \"It accentuates the individuality of the card.\"\n\n> \"I like the different greens. People can pick their own colors.\"\n\n## Technical Implementation\n\n```javascript\n// Example: User-defined section colors\nconst sectionColors = {\n  systemRole: user.preferences?.colors?.systemRole || '#4a7c59',\n  userRole: user.preferences?.colors?.userRole || '#6b8e7f',\n  constraints: user.preferences?.colors?.constraints || '#87a96b',\n  context: user.preferences?.colors?.context || '#a8c09a',\n  // Each user gets their own palette\n};\n```\n\n## The Bottom Line\n\nThis isn't about making everything look the same. It's about creating a workspace that feels **personal, alive, and uniquely yours**. The mixed greens aren't a bug to be fixed - they're the soul of the interface.\n\n**The patchwork quilt is the point.**\n\n---\n\n*\"Why does everything have to be the same shade of corporate blue?\"*\n*- Because it doesn't.*"